, "x": 221.9, "y": -1510.0, "path": [[221.9, -1510.0, 91.0], [211.29, -1508.06, 90.86], [205.12, -1506.85, 90.48], [200.47, -1505.86, 89.77], [197.35, -1505.12, 88.84], [194.85, -1504.47, 87.52], [193.13, -1503.95, 86.04], [191.78, -1503.48, 84.14], [190.91, -1503.1, 82.14], [190.31, -1502.75, 79.74], [190.04, -1502.47, 77.32], [190.0, -1502.21, 74.52], [190.18, -1502.0, 71.78], [190.56, -1501.81, 68.67], [191.07, -1501.65, 65.71], [191.77, -1501.51, 62.41], [192.53, -1501.4, 59.31], [193.45, -1501.3, 55.89], [194.39, -1501.22, 52.7], [195.48, -1501.16, 49.22], [196.54, -1501.1, 45.99], [197.74, -1501.06, 42.48], [198.88, -1501.02, 39.23], [200.14, -1500.99, 35.71], [201.32, -1500.97, 32.47], [202.6, -1500.95, 28.96], [203.8, -1500.94, 25.72], [205.08, -1500.93, 22.22], [206.26, -1500.93, 18.99], [207.53, -1500.92, 15.5], [208.67, -1500.92, 12.28], [209.88, -1500.92, 8.76], [210.97, -1500.93, 5.45], [212.09, -1500.93, 1.78], [212.61, -1500.93, 0.0]]}, {"target": 4, "heading": 27.0, "altitude": 106.0, "speed": 60.0, "x": -1512.0, "y": -1220.2, "path": [[-1512.0, -1220.2, 106.0], [-1505.99, -1209.17, 105.71], [-1501.6, -1202.07, 105.02], [-1497.51, -1196.17, 103.88], [-1494.13, -1191.77, 102.49], [-1490.77, -1187.8, 100.65], [-1487.87, -1184.67, 98.66], [-1484.91, -1181.76, 96.21], [-1482.32, -1179.43, 93.7], [-1479.64, -1177.23, 90.73], [-1477.27, -1175.47, 87.79], [-1474.81, -1173.81, 84.43], [-1472.62, -1172.48, 81.19], [-1470.33, -1171.23, 77.57], [-1468.29, -1170.24, 74.16], [-1466.15, -1169.32, 70.43], [-1464.24, -1168.59, 66.96], [-1462.22, -1167.92, 63.19], [-1460.42, -1167.39, 59.71], [-1458.51, -1166.9, 55.96], [-1456.79, -1166.51, 52.51], [-1454.98, -1166.16, 48.79], [-1453.35, -1165.88, 45.37], [-1451.62, -1165.62, 41.69], [-1450.06, -1165.43, 38.32], [-1448.41, -1165.24, 34.68], [-1446.92, -1165.1, 31.34], [-1445.35, -1164.97, 27.75], [-1443.94, -1164.87, 24.44], [-1442.44, -1164.78, 20.88], [-1441.1, -1164.71, 17.6], [-1439.7, -1164.64, 14.06], [-1438.44, -1164.6, 10.79], [-1437.13, -1164.55, 7.21], [-1435.98, -1164.52, 3.84], [-1434.79, -1164.49, 0.09], [-1434.76, -1164.49, 0.0]]}, {"target": 5, "heading": 105.0, "altitude": 67.0, "speed": 50.0, "x": 881.6, "y": 1229.0, "path": [[881.6, 1229.0, 67.0], [892.32, 1226.2, 66.7], [900.06, 1224.27, 65.98], [907.05, 1222.61, 64.79], [912.65, 1221.35, 63.34], [918.03, 1220.18, 61.43], [922.5, 1219.26, 59.36], [926.9, 1218.38, 56.85], [930.62, 1217.69, 54.31], [934.32, 1217.03, 51.38], [937.46, 1216.5, 48.56], [940.61, 1216.01, 45.39], [943.3, 1215.61, 42.4], [946.01, 1215.24, 39.11], [948.34, 1214.94, 36.04], [950.71, 1214.66, 32.69], [952.77, 1214.44, 29.59], [954.86, 1214.23, 26.23], [956.69, 1214.06, 23.13], [958.56, 1213.9, 19.77], [960.2, 1213.77, 16.67], [961.89, 1213.65, 13.32], [963.36, 1213.56, 10.21], [964.87, 1213.46, 6.78], [966.19, 1213.39, 3.53], [967.49, 1213.32, 0.0]]}]}, "agent_error": null, "agent_extra": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "served_by": ["claude-opus-5-5"], "fallbacks": false, "thinking": {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}, "effort": "high", "notes": [{"before_call": 1, "text": "I'm starting by pulling up the mission briefing."}, {"before_call": 2, "text": "I'm settling on an approach: the wind can be estimated from the aircraft's own measurements, using test drops at several altitudes to build a wind profile, with drops outside the smoke giving drag and drops through it giving updraft. I'm now thinking about how hotspots relate to the fires and smoke columns, so I can place the drops.\n\nI'm planning the next test drops: one at 100 m altitude well clear of the smoke, then more at other altitudes away from it, and a few through the smoke afterward. I'm setting the first one at a point about 400 m north, heading 0 at 60 m/s, so it should land roughly 250 m on and outside the smoke."}, {"before_call": 3, "text": "The drag here is clearly strong: the projectile only covered 61 m horizontally over a 7.82 s fall, against 4.5 s in a vacuum. That points to a small terminal velocity, so I'm estimating the drag coefficient at around 0.06, which gives roughly 12.8 m/s and lines up with the timing. I also have a wind component of about 6.7 m/s toward the east to account for.\n\nI'm checking the wind effect at 100 m, and now I'm moving on to the drop points at 30 m and 150 m."}, {"before_call": 5, "text": "I'm checking the wind readings at 30, 100 and 150 meters, then working out the shear exponents between each pair, which comes out to about 0.33 on average with essentially no veer. Next I want to look at the smoke drift to estimate the updraft, starting from a baseline hypothesis.\n\nI'm now running the baseline values through the model: a drag coefficient of 0.06, wind speed 6.7, wind direction 92, shear 0.33, no veer, and an updraft of 5."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "The fall times look too long, which points to drag being set too high. The test1 landing comes up short of the target forward distance, so I think drag is too large. I'll try 0.04."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "I'm still finding the drag a bit high, since the dt is positive. Working out the sensitivity from the first test, a small change in drag shifts dt by roughly 0.058 per 0.001, and I'm aiming for a -0.15 shift, which puts the drag near 0.0375. The second test gives a similar estimate of about 0.037, and the third test has no sensitivity to work from.\n\nI'm settling on a drag of about 0.037, since the second test's crosswind offset suggests the wind has a slightly more northward component. Trying wind direction 90 with that drag."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "The remaining offset is negative in every test, so I need more eastward wind. Tests at altitudes 100, 150 and 30 are short by 2.2, 3.9 and 1.5. With drag high, the load roughly follows the wind, so I'll raise wind speed by about 0.3 and see if test 1 gains around 2.2. I'll try a wind speed of 7.0.\n\nFor test 3, the fall time is 3.3 seconds, so drift is less sensitive to wind there, and I'll try the same wind speed of 7.0 to see the effect."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "I'm checking that the last result falls within noise, and now moving on to the updraft and the fine wind profile. Shear and veer are harder to pin down, so I'm thinking about extra test drops at different altitudes and headings. Next I want to test smoke drops, letting the load spend time inside the smoke column so the data is more informative.\n\nI'm working out where to release a test drop so it lands on the fire center: the horizontal drift decays quickly, so most of the travel happens in the first second. Test 1 put the load about 41.6 m east and 61 m north of the target, so I'm offsetting the release point by that amount to correct for the wind, and I'll keep in mind that any updraft will make the landing differ."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "The fall time came out at 9.04 against 7.82, so the updraft is clearly slowing the descent. I'll set it beside updraft 5 to see how the two compare."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "The updraft needs to drop. At 5 it leaves dt at 0.51, and working from the no-updraft baseline I estimate roughly 0.35 seconds per metre per second, so zero should be near 3.5. I'll try that."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "I'm working out the interpolated value, roughly 3.86, and will use 3.9. The 2-unit offset in test 4 pointed further north than predicted, but at 1.5 m that could just be noise, so I'm treating it as acceptable. Next I'm looking at the main uncertainties, veer and shear, along with the wind measurement noise and the hotspot altitude range.\n\nNext I'm planning six more test drops at different altitudes and headings, with smoke drops at lower altitude to check whether the updraft matters more there. I'm also noting that near terminal velocity the updraft directly cuts the fall speed, so the 3.9 m/s value is significant.\n\nI'm laying out six more test drops: smoke drops at 40 m and 150 m, plus two non-smoke drops at 70 m and 125 m from opposite corners, with two spares held back. The non-smoke ones don't need simulation, so I'll set those up first."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "I'm checking the trajectory numbers: an alt-150 shot at heading 270 and speed 70 lands about 5.2 units east of the release point, so the wind roughly cancels the forward motion. To hit the origin I'd need to release at (-5.2, 0) instead. Smoke would stretch the fall and push the drift further east, which is useful to know regardless. Next I'm working out the alt-40, heading-45 case, which lands offset at (52.4, 38.7) and would need a release at (-52.4, -38.7).\n\nI'm comparing the wind measurements against the model, which predicts about 6.25 at 70m and 7.5 at 125m. The measured values, including 8.1 at 125m, look higher than that, so I'll check all the data points together before drawing a conclusion."}, {"before_call": 19, "text": "I'm going through the test residuals: test 7 is off by about -4.7 in x, a long fall in smoke that was predicted less eastward, and test 5 is off by -2.3 with some along-track and cross error. Tests 4 and 8 both show the prediction running too far north, so I'm wondering whether wind_dir should be a bit more than 90, but test 1's dy of +1.0 doesn't fit that\n\nI'm testing whether a slightly faster, more steeply sheared wind profile fits the measured winds at each altitude, and it does: a base speed of 7.2 with shear 0.33 tracks the readings from 30 to 150 m closely. For direction, the residuals point to a value a bit above 90, around 91.5, though test 1 doesn't agree with that, so I'm still weighing it against the drag adjustment for test 6.\n\nI'm checking test 6's fall time, which is about 0.16 short, roughly three times the noise level. I'm wondering whether the stronger relative wind at altitude could add enough drag to explain it, though I suspect the effect is small."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "I'm looking at the high-altitude tests, where the predicted values run too far south, which suggests the direction should shift toward the north as altitude increases. At low altitude the prediction seems to need more southward bearing, so I'm considering whether the veer should be negative. The measured value of 93 at high altitude sits within a 4\u00b0 noise band, and the impact data seems more precise, though I'm still checking test 2, which was heading 90.\n\nI'm comparing the per-test sensitivity of the residuals to wind direction, which runs about -0.3 to -1.5 m per degree depending on the test, and I'm checking whether a direction near 91 with a small negative veer would fit both the high- and low-altitude cases. Test 1 and test 4 at 100 m both show positive residuals at 90\u00b0, which points to a direction near 90 at that altitude.\n\nI'm settling on a wind direction of about 91.5\u00b0 with a veer of roughly -4 per 100 m, since the residuals at 100 m and 150 m point to that trend. The measured 93 at high altitude is a mild conflict, but the noise band is 4\u00b0, so I'm treating it as compatible. I'm now turning to the x residuals at 7.2 m/s, starting with test 7 at high altitude heading west.\n\nI'm checking the x residuals at high altitude, where test 2 and test 7 both show the predicted along-track displacement running too large, which points toward slightly more drag. Test 6 also has a fall time residual that suggests the prediction is short, so a bit more drag would help there too.\n\nI'm weighing whether more drag would fix the along-track overshoot at high altitude, and I'm noting that a drag bump around 0.038 would add only about 0.1 s to the fall. I'm also considering whether an updraft could explain test 7's short east drift, though that would mean more drift for test 2 too.\n\nI'm going to test a few hypotheses next, starting with a drag of 0.037, a wind speed of 7.2, a direction of 91.5\u00b0, shear of 0.33, a veer of -4, and an updraft of 3.9."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "I'm checking the veer adjustment: with a -4 veer and the pivot at 100, the low levels shift toward the south and the high levels toward the north, so the dy at t2 and t7 should rise, but the tweak I applied only moved them by +0.3 and +0.2. The low levels dropped by about 1, and the t2 case at 150 looks like it should land near the ws7.0 dir-90 result of about -0.7, yet I'm getting -2.2.\n\nThe t2 result at dir 89.5 still doesn't match the ws 7.0 dir-90 value of -0.7, so the offset must depend on the direction averaged over the fall rather than the surface direction alone. Across all eight tests the mean dy is now about -1.2, which points to reducing the mean direction, though the spread looks like noise at roughly 1.5 m per axis, so I shouldn't over-fit the veer.\n\nNow I'm settling on a ws value of about 7.15, since the 7.2 and 7.0 fits bracket it. The test-by-test dx pattern looks like noise around that. I can't keep running test drops once calibration ends, so I need to pick the last two now, aiming for a slow, high-altitude case heading south through the smoke.\n\nI'm planning the last two test drops now, since calibration ends once I begin operation. I want one at altitude 120 heading south at speed 80, and one at altitude 60 heading northwest at speed 45. I'll simulate both release points using the current best fit: drag 0.037, surface wind 7.15, direction 91, shear 0.33, veer 0, and updraft 3.9."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "I'm working out the release offsets for t9 and t10, which are just the negatives of their original vectors."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "I'm checking tests 9 and 10: the predicted position is close to the observed one, though I'm not sure the numbers line up, since the compare step reports a slightly different offset. The gap seems to come from smoke adding an eastward drift that my release calculation left out, so the two are consistent. The residuals look like plain noise, with a mean miss around 1.6 m.\n\nThe residuals already look like they're sitting at the noise floor, so fur